    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d681">

                <pb facs="1239" n="1223" sameAs="frus:frus1950v04/pg1223"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">124.613/8–150: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Embassy in the Soviet
                        Union </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-08-07T17:00:00-04:00">August 7, 1950—5
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>104. Embtel 257 Aug 1.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d681-f1"/> As Emb aware Sov Govt on
                    past occasions has attempted to identify Rev John Brassard with Emb and USGov.
                    We have continued maintain position that Brassard is, as were his predecessors,
                    private Amer citizen who proceeded to and resident in USSR by virtue provisions
                    Roosevelt Litvinov agreement.</p>
                <p> Emb therefore not authorized employ Brassard. Dept desires no indication be
                    given that USGov in any way has moderated its position or accepts Sov
                    pretentions Brassard has official status.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d681-f1" n="1" xml:id="d681-f1-content">
                <p>As Father Brassard had encountered continued delay from the Soviet authorities in
                    performing his religious functions, the Embassy in this telegram, not printed,
                    had asked for authority to employ Father Brassard as a part time general clerk
                    for duty in the Embassy library at a stipend of $2850. (124.613/8–150)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
